A movie frame written at zlib level 1 with the Sub filter, applied by scripts/prepare.sh to every slice. Stock
Godot's `--write-movie` to a `.png` writes each frame through `Image::save_png_to_buffer`, libpng's simplified writer
at its default (zlib level 6, every filter tried per row): on a 1080x1920 frame that is about 0.4 s of one core, and
the whole cost of a composition render once the GPU draws the frame. This writes the same pixels (PNG is lossless)
at level 1 with the Sub filter, about seven times faster and a quarter larger on disk. Only `MovieWriterPNGWAV::
write_frame` calls it, only RGB8 and RGBA8 take the fast path (any other format takes the stock encoder), and every
other PNG save, the editor and the exported pack's own `save_png` included, is unchanged. Template builds only.

